 based solutions across the globe  sentence platform analyzes and fuses  data from different iot devices to  deliver insights across multiple  industries  they said  with compute engine and cloud tpu  sensing can spin up vms and configure  tpu clusters on demand with minimal  effort  and expanding capacity or extending  functionality is fast and easy  i am going to demonstrate how to create  a compute engine manage instance group  to deploy a simple web application  docker will be used to build the  container images and deploy them in our  virtual machines  we're also going to expose our  application to the internet by using an  external http load balancer  our sample application is a simple  guestbook where visitors can submit  messages and clear them we can perform  these steps by both using the google  cloud sdk in the cloud shell or using  the google cloud console  before we get to deploy our app i will  use cloud shell to clone the github repo  and use the text editor to show you the  code  you can find the link to the github repo  in the video description if you want to  follow along  let's click the activate cloud shell  button at the top of the google cloud  console  and we see the cloud shell section open  inside a new frame at the bottom of the  console and display a command line  prompt  let's use this terminal to enter the get  clone command and clone the repo  cloud shell comes with a built-in code  editor that we'll use to read the code  launch the cloud shell editor by  clicking the code editor button on the  toolbar of the cloud shelf window  now let's use the left hand pane to  navigate to the code by clicking on the  cpu 100 folder  then app folder and select the app.pi  file  as you can see in this python script  flask is used for handling http requests  for publishing and clearing messages and  redis is used to save the entries  we also have a docker file containing  all the commands to create the images  that will be used to run our docker  container  i am now going to show you how to create  an instance template  instance templates are a convenient way  to save a virtual machine's instance  configuration so you can use it later to  create a virtual machine or a group of  virtual machines  let's navigate to compute engine using  the left hand pane and click instance  templates  click create instance template  and it is now time to enter our desired  configuration  let's give it a simple name  guestbook server  under the machine configuration section  we can see virtual machines are  classified by machine family series and  machine types  for our application let's select the  general purpose e2 medium  which we can see has two vcpus and four  gigs of memory  under the boot this section we can click  the change button to select the  operating system in its version and the  disk type and size we want for our  virtual machines  let's add our startup script which will  install docker  build the image  deploy the container  and expose it internally  our individual machines don't need an  external ip because we will expose the  app to the internet using a load  balancer  so let's specify none as the external ip  and finally let's click create  now that we have our template  let's use it to create a managed  instance group  manage instance groups lets you operate  apps on multiple identical vms and make  your workload scalable and highly  available  you can take advantage of automated  services including auto scaling auto  healing multiple zone deployment and  automatic updating  from the instance templates section  expand the action menu next to the  guestbook server template  and click the create instance group  button  we now see the configuration options for  a group of vms  let's give this group a simple name like  guestbook server mid  and we can now decide if we want our vms  to be deployed in one or multiple zones  we will keep this in the us central one  a zone  notice we can configure the auto scaling  mode and policy a minimum and maximum  number of vms  auto scaling metrics auto healing and  other aspects of our managed instance  group  let's go ahead and click create  now if we navigate to the vm instances  menu  we're able to see all the individual  machines that are part of our management  group  let's go ahead and check that docker is  running correctly via ssh  click ssh under the connect column  corresponding to the only instance in a  group in the instances list  to ssh in a new window  we'll use the command  sudo  systemctl  status docker  in the resulting output we can see the  container engine is active and running  our container as intended  before creating our load balancer we  need to create an ingress firewall rule  applicable to the group of vms being  load balanced to allow health checks  let's use cloud shell to create this  rule  notice the two source ip ranges and  protocol needed to allow health checks  now let's create a configuration by  selecting the backend services input box  and click the create a back-end service  button  let's give it the name  guestbook back-end  and let's choose  guest book server mig instance group as  our backend  enter 80 for the port number  our backend requires us to create a  health check so traffic is only directed  to healthy machines so let's select the  health check input box and click the  create l check  i will name this  basic http health check  and leave the default values  select our newly created health check  and click create  now let's review the front-end default  configuration  and we can see the protocol will be http  and a female ipv4 ip address will be  assigned  and port 80 will be used  everything looks good  so we can click on the create button  let's review some of our load balancer  details by clicking on the guestbook  http lb  in the load balancers list  and we're now able to use this ip  address to use the web application  running in our manage instance group  let's use the browser to visit the  application by copying and pasting the  ip address and port in the address bar  and here it is our simple guestbook  application  now let's leave a short message in our  guestbook  hello  google cloud  and we're set  we deployed our guesswork application to  a manchester's group and exposed it  using a load balancer  and if after the session you cannot get  enough of compute engine and are keen to  find out more  check out the suggestions here and the  links in the description box below  you can read up on compute engine  you can check out the google cloud  drawing board on compute engine  or you can try our quick labs on compute  engine in our next episode you'll be  learning about google cloud's global  network it will cover vpc networking  firewall rules  load balancers a demo where we'll  configure the network for the booking  application you saw earlier finally  another customer success story  thank you and we'll see you in the next  video  [Music]

